\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We studied vision token pruning across seven VLA models (450M--7B) spanning three connector families on closed-loop robotic manipulation. Four findings emerge. (1)~The vision connector, not model scale or action head, determines pruning behavior along a four-class compressibility spectrum: uniformly critical (linear projection), threshold-graded (linear + deep post-processing), smooth graded (MLP), and catastrophic cliff (pixel-shuffle). The ordering is non-monotonic with parameter count, and controlled comparisons (same encoder, same token count, different connector) isolate the connector as causal. (2)~Low-CV models are uniformly critical, not immune. $\pi_0.5$ retains 69.4\% success rate under random removal at $k{=}0.5$ but collapses to 33.4\% under L2 norm selection; this 36\,pp gap falsifies the ``immune'' interpretation from $\pi_0$'s offline-only evaluation and establishes that method selection, not just retention ratio, determines whether compression succeeds. (3)~Token importance CV serves as a zero-cost dual predictor: it forecasts both the tolerance class and the optimal pruning strategy from a single forward pass. The gap between random and L2 norm selection scales monotonically with CV at both $k{=}0.5$ and $k{=}0.7$, validated across three models. (4)~VTR re-encoding overcomes the catastrophic cliff: both cross-attention and parameter-matched MLP modules recover 60\%+ success rate at 75\% compression where all deletion methods yield 0\%, with a flat M-curve ($M{=}16 \approx M{=}128$).

These results yield a Diagnose-then-Compress workflow. First, compute CV from a single forward pass on task-relevant frames. Models below CV $\approx 0.15$ should use coverage-preserving methods (random or spatially uniform selection); models above this threshold can use importance-based scoring; pixel-shuffle architectures (CV $> 0.3$) require learned re-encoding rather than deletion. The CV diagnostic replaces hundreds of expensive closed-loop evaluation episodes with a gradient-free computation.

Limitations remain. All closed-loop results use the LIBERO simulation benchmark; real-world deployment conditions (sensor noise, contact dynamics) may alter the compressibility spectrum. $\pi_0$ evaluation is offline only; closed-loop verification may reveal degradation not captured by single-step loss. The CV scaling law is validated at three models and two retention ratios; extending to additional architectures and benchmarks is necessary before formal quantitative use. Code and evaluation scripts will be released upon publication.
